# The reference flags of lit parameter msil_references (plan 5.11.10, 5.12.2,
# 5.12.3; M21): %{msil_ref_flags} is -Wl,--reference=<dir> and part of
# %{msil_ldflags}, so it reaches the MSIL side of every link and never the
# native oracle. Nested lits run the suite prog/Inputs/nested/refs, in which
# prog/Inputs/prog-cc.py stands in for clang and for msil-ld behind it and
# records the arguments of each link (prog.exe.inputs, which refs-prog.test
# prints after its composite); refs-features.test echoes the features and
# substitutions. Every nested run passes an empty msil_linux_sysroot, so
# %{msil_ldflags} holds nothing but %{msil_ref_flags} on either triple.
# (1) msil_references=<a directory> under obj: the MSIL link of %{msil_prog}
#     (refs-prog.test) and of the one-source %{msil_c} (refs-c.c) passes
#     -Wl,--reference=<the directory>, which the stand-in msil-ld records;
#     the native oracle's link (a Windows triple on the local runner, where
#     the stand-in's native program runs) does not; both programs pass; the
#     feature is msil-ref=path, and %{msil_ref_flags} and %{msil_ldflags}
#     echo the flag;
# (2) msil_references=none (the default) under obj: no link has --reference,
#     the feature is msil-ref=none, and %{msil_ref_flags} and %{msil_ldflags}
#     are empty (the expansion of M15 with this sysroot);
# (3) under lto the MSIL link gets the flag as well;
# (4) packs named by $MSIL_REF_NET48, $MSIL_REF_NET8 and $MSIL_REF_NET10
#     (directories that hold a file named like the pack's core library) give
#     the features msil-refpack=<name> and %{msil_ref_dir_<name>}, and
#     msil_references=net8 links with that directory (msil-ref=net8);
# (5) %msil_check_mscorlib_safe runs check-mscorlib-safe.py with this
#     configuration's msil-ld and ilasm and the runtimes of the run, which
#     name the programs' own runtime as well: with msil_dotnet_runtime=9
#     (where .NET 9 is installed) --runtimes has net9, though it is neither
#     .NET 8 nor .NET 10, so a host with only .NET 9 never gets an empty list;
# (6) configuration errors, each with lit's fatal-error status 2: a value
#     other than none under msil_pipeline=asm (asm has no link), a path that
#     does not exist, an explicitly named pack directory without its core
#     library (also when msil_references is none), msil_run_netfx=1 with
#     net8 (where the .NET Framework is installed: images bound to .NET 8
#     references do not load there), net10 with msil_dotnet_runtime=8
#     (where .NET 8 is installed), and msil_equiv=1 under msil_pipeline=lto
#     with net8 or the path of a .NET pack (O4 would compare the unbound
#     --lto-emit-asm text with an image whose data-import helper calls
#     NativeLibrary), while net48 and none load under the same parameters;
# (7) a path naming a .NET pack, System.Runtime.dll or a directory holding
#     it (stand-ins that ilasm assembles from an Assembly row alone), is
#     handled as net<N>, N being that row's major version: with 8.0.0.0
#     (a directory, and the file itself) %msil_verify has the pack's
#     directory as --runtime-dir and --skip-peverify "bound to .NET 8
#     references"; with 10.0.0.0, msil_run_netfx=1 (where the .NET Framework
#     is installed) and msil_dotnet_runtime=8 (where .NET 8 is installed) are
#     the errors of net10; with the .NET Framework facade's 4.1.2.0 the path
#     is any other path (PEVerify stays, the Framework run loads); a
#     System.Runtime.dll whose version cannot be read is an error naming it.
# REQUIRES: msil-ld, msil-ar

# RUN: rm -rf %t && mkdir -p %t/pack %t/empty %t/fake48 %t/fake8 %t/fake10
# RUN: %{harness_check} write %t/fake48/mscorlib.dll
# RUN: %{harness_check} write %t/fake8/System.Runtime.dll
# RUN: %{harness_check} write %t/fake10/System.Runtime.dll
# RUN: mkdir -p %t/sr8 %t/sr10 %t/srfx %t/srbad
# RUN: %{harness_check} write %t/sr8.il ".assembly System.Runtime { .ver 8:0:0:0 }"
# RUN: %{harness_check} write %t/sr10.il ".assembly System.Runtime { .ver 10:0:0:0 }"
# RUN: %{harness_check} write %t/srfx.il ".assembly System.Runtime { .ver 4:1:2:0 }"
# RUN: %ilasm -dll -output=%t/sr8/System.Runtime.dll %t/sr8.il
# RUN: %ilasm -dll -output=%t/sr10/System.Runtime.dll %t/sr10.il
# RUN: %ilasm -dll -output=%t/srfx/System.Runtime.dll %t/srfx.il
# RUN: %{harness_check} write %t/srbad/System.Runtime.dll

